.TH DEVELOP 1
.SH NAME
develop \- edit, rebuild, and relaunch a running Limbo program
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wm/develop
.I source
.RI [ "dis" " [ "arg ..." ]" ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Develop
is opened by the pencil button in an application window's titlebar.
The window manager plumbs the program's primary Limbo source, resolved Dis
module, and original argument vector to a new development window.
.PP
When current symbol information is available, the main text area opens at the
exact source statement of the first application frame on the captured live
stack.  It falls back to the program's primary
.B .b
file.  The
.B Modules
menu lists that file, its included
.B .m
declarations, and conventionally named implementation sources found in the
standard Inferno source directories.
.PP
.I Source
may have the form
.IB file : line .
Develop opens the file with the insertion point at that line.  Plumbing rules
may therefore select a source statement by starting Develop with a
.IB file : line
argument.  Titlebar requests also include the running process identifier;
when debugging symbols describe its current frame, Develop automatically
opens that frame and selects its current source line.
.PP
When Develop is entered from a running application, it briefly stops the
captured Dis process, records its symbolic call stack, arguments, and local
values using the existing Dis and
.B .sbl
debug information, and resumes it.  The snapshot remains fixed while source
is edited, so navigation does not silently change the state being examined.
The same snapshot records modules on the captured call stack and non-nil
module references in its symbolic module globals.  Their loaded Dis paths are
mapped back to implementation sources and retained in the session's
.B Modules
menu and definition index.  Thus button-3 lookup reflects the modules loaded
by that application instance, not merely files named by textual includes.
For a built-in module, lookup prefers its native runtime implementation; for
example, looking up Math member
.B cos
opens
.B Math_cos
in
.B /libinterp/math.c
instead of stopping at the declaration in
.BR /module/math.m .
System calls are likewise mapped past the module registrar to their hosted
implementation; for example,
.B sys->bind
opens
.B Sys_bind
in
.BR /emu/port/inferno.c .
.PP
Button 3 on a Limbo identifier recursively follows the program's included
module declarations and their corresponding implementation sources, looking
for a matching function, method, type, or variable declaration.  Function
bodies are preferred to interface declarations.  Develop opens the defining file and selects the complete
declaration line.  If there is no local definition, it plumbs the identifier
from the current source directory so site-specific rules or another tool can
handle it.
.PP
The commands are:
.TP
.B Save
Write the current source file.
.TP
.B Undo
Undo the last textual change.  Control-underscore invokes the same command.
.TP
.B Build
Save the current file and compile the primary source with debugging symbols,
writing the result over the program's resolved Dis module.
.TP
.B Run
Launch the current Dis module with the argument vector captured when the
original application was started.
.TP
.B Go
Save and build, terminate the captured old application only after a successful
build, and relaunch it with its original arguments.
.TP
.B State
Display the call stack, arguments, and locals recorded when Develop opened.
.PP
Compiler diagnostics are written to the window-system console; build status
is also shown at the bottom of the editor.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /module
Standard module declaration directory searched for relative includes.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/wm/develop.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR limbo (1),
.IR plumb (1),
.IR wmclient (2),
.IR plumbing (6)
